Fix the available-memory reading and make the guard proportional

The guard refused safe runs, reporting "71 MB available" on an 8 GB machine.
Two bugs:

1. `vm.page_inactive_count` is not exposed on modern macOS, so that term
   silently fell back to zero and discarded the largest reclaimable chunk.
2. Reading only `vm.page_free_count` is wrong by design: macOS keeps almost no
   memory free, it fills RAM with file cache and reclaims on demand. A healthy
   machine legitimately shows under 100 MB free.

The reading now goes through Mach `host_statistics64`, summing free, inactive,
speculative and purgeable pages: the memory the kernel can hand out without
swapping. Same machine now reports 3.0 GB instead of 71 MB.

The guard is also proportional instead of absolute:

- **Block** only when the run exceeds **total physical memory**. That is the
  case that rebooted the machine (6.7 GB of blocks on 8 GB); it cannot fit at
  all and the swap storm is a real risk.
- **Warn** when it exceeds what is free right now. Transient overshoot is what
  swap is for: slower, not fatal. Blocking on it made the guard unusable, since
  a run sized to fit in RAM would be refused as soon as a browser was open.

Adds `diag_memory_info`, which prints the three figures the guard uses so they
can be checked against `vm_stat`.
